artifact.self_contained_problem:
Construct a single, fully coherent theoretical framework for heavy-quarkonium production in nucleus–nucleus collisions that simultaneously describes all available data on the J/ψ family (J/ψ, ψ(2S), χ_c) and the Υ family (Υ(1S), Υ(2S), Υ(3S), χ_b) across the SPS, RHIC, and LHC center-of-mass energies. The framework must consistently incorporate, within one calculation:

1. Lattice-QCD spectral functions for the in-medium quarkonium states (encoding screening and thermal widths as functions of temperature T);
2. Cold nuclear matter effects, including nuclear modifications of parton distribution functions (shadowing/antishadowing/EMC), nuclear absorption, and Cronin-type transverse-momentum broadening;
3. Parton energy loss in cold and hot nuclear matter;
4. Co-mover (hadronic and partonic comover) interactions;
5. Hydrodynamic evolution of the quark–gluon plasma background (with realistic initial conditions and equation of state);
6. Heavy-quark diffusion in the QGP, governed by a transport (drag/diffusion) coefficient, and the resulting regeneration of quarkonia from uncorrelated and correlated Q\bar{Q} pairs;
7. Feed-down contributions from higher excited states to the observed ground-state yields.

Within this framework, determine the nuclear modification factors R_AA (and elliptic flow v_2 where applicable) for each charmonium and bottomonium state as functions of collision energy √s_NN, centrality, transverse momentum, and rapidity, and answer the following specific question: does the sequential melting pattern of the Υ family, R_AA[Υ(1S)] > R_AA[Υ(2S)] > R_AA[Υ(3S)], change quantitatively between RHIC (√s_NN ≈ 200 GeV) and LHC (√s_NN ≈ 5 TeV) energies once all of the above ingredients are included, in a way that is consistent with measured data?

